00:18:48.436 --> 00:18:48.796
<v Maya>Right.</v>

144
00:18:49.257 --> 00:19:00.363
<v Daniel>Conditional on being employed at an adopting firm, the very workers whose entry-level jobs appear to be quietly vanishing from the broader economy are the exact same workers using the tool the hardest.</v>

145
00:19:00.638 --> 00:19:15.605
<v Maya>So what does this actually mean? Because the implications are wildly different depending on how you read it. Is this heavy junior use, jumping use by which these young workers become productive enough to keep their jobs? Are they looking around at the hiring freezes, realizing the bar has been raised and thinking, I have to use AI</v>

146
00:19:15.645 --> 00:19:18.426
<v Maya>for eight extra tasks a week just to survive the cuts?</v>

147
00:19:19.087 --> 00:19:32.155
<v Daniel>Or is it the exact opposite mechanism? Are they using AI so heavily and becoming so hyperproductive that one entry-level kid is now easily absorbing the workload of what used to be three entry-level hires?</v>

148
00:19:32.355 --> 00:19:33.415
<v Maya>Oh, man. Right.</v>

149
00:19:33.495 --> 00:19:39.137
<v Daniel>If that is the case, their heavy use is effectively destroying the future junior jobs for their peers.</v>

150
00:19:39.788 --> 00:19:51.684
<v Maya>It feels like the defining economic question of this decade. So looking at the data from OpenAI, Columbia and Wharton, which one is it? Are they surviving or are they automating away their peers?</v>

151
00:19:51.724 --> 00:19:55.970
<v Daniel>Well, the truth is we have to be completely intellectually honest with you. The lead paper does not answer that question.</v>

152
00:19:56.150 --> 00:19:56.810
<v Maya>It doesn't. It</v>

153
00:19:56.850 --> 00:20:11.637
<v Daniel>can't. The telemetry data is brilliant for showing us the intensity of use. It shows us exactly what tasks are consuming the most volume, but it cannot tell us the macroeconomic destiny of that 23-year-old analyst sending all those prompts. It leaves us sitting with an</v>

154
00:20:11.797 --> 00:20:15.379
<v Daniel>unresolved, incredibly profound tension about the future of work.</v>

155
00:20:15.678 --> 00:20:30.389
<v Maya>Which honestly changes how I visualize a corporate office right now. You have the executives feeling like they've successfully deployed AI because they log in to draft a quick email once a week. And meanwhile, down in the trenches, the youngest workers are practically plugged into the</v>

156
00:20:30.429 --> 00:20:39.596
<v Maya>matrix, churning out complex documentation and technical work at light speed, while the empty desks next to them just stay empty because the company quietly stopped hiring.</v>

157
00:20:39.816 --> 00:20:52.103
<v Daniel>Yeah. If we pull back from the debate on junior workers for a moment, the ultimate takeaway synthesized by the researchers across all this data is a crucial fundamental distinction. Adoption is not deployment.</v>

158
00:20:52.523 --> 00:20:54.444
<v Maya>Buying the gym membership is not working out.</v>

159
00:20:54.504 --> 00:21:09.252
<v Daniel>Precisely. Firms are not just flipping a switch and deciding whether to use generative AI. They're undergoing a very slow, very messy, very human process of learning where it actually belongs in their daily workflows. Right. And this leads to a sobering implication for the broader</v>

160
00:21:09.292 --> 00:21:20.782
<v Daniel>economy. We established earlier that the biggest, most valuable, most intangible rich firms are the ones adopting earliest and integrating best because they have the SG&A infrastructure to support the J curve.</v>

161
00:21:21.203 --> 00:21:36.166
<v Maya>So if a broadly available technology like ChatGPT is integrated most effectively by the absolute titans of industry, it could actually widen the gap between superstar firms and the rest of the economy. We always love to think of software as a</v>

162
00:21:36.206 --> 00:21:43.072
<v Maya>great democratizing force. But in this early phase, it might actually be an engine for inequality between companies.</v>

163
00:21:43.152 --> 00:21:44.973
<v Daniel>Exactly. That's a very real possibility.</v>

164
00:21:45.133 --> 00:21:59.984
<v Maya>Before we wrap this up, we need to explicitly run through the limitations of everything we've discussed today just to keep us all grounded in reality. The researchers are very transparent about the scope of their data, and we need to be, too. First, this analysis is based on ChatGPT</v>

165
00:22:00.085 --> 00:22:10.834
<v Maya>Enterprise only. Right. It does not include Microsoft Copilot. It does not include Anthropix Cloud. It doesn't include API usage, in-house custom-built tools, or anyone using personal accounts at work.</v>

166
00:22:11.334 --> 00:22:25.853
<v Daniel>Second, the job title coverage in their data set is incomplete. We have the breakdown of who is using it by title, but we don't have the denominator, meaning we don't know the total number of employees in those specific roles across all 1,700 companies. Because of that, we don't have a</v>

167
00:22:26.033 --> 00:22:28.796
<v Daniel>true, precise parole adoption rate.</v>

168
00:22:29.096 --> 00:22:43.247
<v Maya>Third, and this is crucial, message counts measure intensity. They do not measure economic value. Oh, absolutely. One brilliant strategic prompt from an executive that redirects a product launch might generate a million dollars of value,</v>

169
00:22:43.607 --> 00:22:51.333
<v Maya>while 10 rapid-fire prompts from a trainee might just be fixing a formatting error in a spreadsheet. Volume does not equal impact.</v>

170
00:22:51.633 --> 00:23:06.422
<v Daniel>And finally, none of the financial results we discussed. The revenue correlations, the market cap advantages are causal. We absolutely cannot say that AI caused those financial outcomes. Only that massive financial scale is strongly associated with early adoption.</v>

171
00:23:07.024 --> 00:23:21.969
<v Maya>The authors readily admit all of this themselves, and our analysis is better for keeping those boundaries clear. But as we look at this unprecedented peak inside the actual real world credit card statement of enterprise AI, I want to leave you with one final provocative thought to mull over.</v>

172
00:23:22.049 --> 00:23:22.389
<v Daniel>Go for it.</v>

173
00:23:22.749 --> 00:23:37.557
<v Maya>We spent a lot of time debating the junior paradox. We see the executives barely scratching the surface of the tool and the entry level trainees using it with massive unprecedented intensity. But if the heavy use of AI by junior employees ends up permanently</v>

174
00:23:37.617 --> 00:23:52.311
<v Maya>shrinking the pipeline of entry-level jobs, if companies just stop hiring as many 22-year-olds because the ones they have are doing the work of three people, where will the next generation of managers and executives come from in 10 years? If you automate away the</v>

175
00:23:52.371 --> 00:23:58.537
<v Maya>entry-level training ground today, who is going to be left with the institutional knowledge to actually run the company tomorrow?</v>

176
00:23:58.877 --> 00:24:02.891
<v Daniel>It is a structural time bomb that just isn't showing up on any corporate balance sheet right now.</v>

177
00:24:03.032 --> 00:24:13.628
<v Maya>Exactly. Thank you so much for joining us on this deep dive into the real data behind the AI revolution. Until next time, keep questioning the surveys and keep looking for the actual numbers.</v>
